A||N||7||fierose||Helm's Manual Question||||1||
Z||000000||fierose||11-26-2000||06:20 PM||||Was checking into getting a Helm's Manual because I heard they were the best documentation you can get.  <A HREF="http://www.helmsinc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.helmsinc.com</A>   The price they had for their manuals were $60.  I've seen them on e-bay going for more than that.  Just wanted to make sure that the shop manuals they sell are actually the manuals made by GM and the best you can buy.  TIA<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-26-2000||06:56 PM||||The 1986 Fiero owner's manual had a form in the back, for ordering a Fiero service manual from Helm, Incorporated of Highland Park, MI.  Part number is (was) S-86-10-P.  <BR>It cost $26 back then.<P>I purchased one, and think it's pretty good.  Lots of illustrations, and component locations that aren't spelled out in the other manuals, etc.  Schematics, and troubleshooting flowcharts are good, too.<BR> <BR>Best bang for the buck, though, is Haynes, IMHO.      <P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||11-26-2000||10:53 PM||||Helms makes the Factory manuals for GM. I have a Factory and a Haynes. The haynes is Very Good on 90% of the work you do on a Fiero. It's the 10% that I use the Factory service manual for (electrical schematics, Extra pics or illustrations, and any single year differences.) If you don't want to pay the high $$$ for the service manual the Haynes is the next best thing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||11-27-2000||12:32 PM||||Spend the extra money and get the Helm's factory manual.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierose||11-27-2000||02:49 PM||||Thanks for the input guys.  Since I'm a broke college student and my car has 230,000 miles on it I'm just going to spend the money since a newer car is no where in my future.||||reg||6
